        "content": "Two independent weaknesses compose the exploit:\n\n1. **Low-entropy reset secret:** a four-digit decimal OTP has at most 10,000 values. The successful `1664` response confirms a token in this format was accepted on the target instance; it does not establish the exact generation algorithm.\n2. **Incorrectly scoped abuse control:** a single GraphQL POST carried 1,000 `resetPassword` invocations and all were executed. The earlier 429 for separate requests, contrasted with successful alias batches, shows that the effective request throttling did not prevent many OTP comparisons within one operation.\n\nGraphQL aliases are a standard feature, not themselves a vulnerability. The issue is the interaction between an attacker-controlled repeated sensitive mutation, an easy-to-enumerate OTP space, and the lack of effective **per-account, per-token, or per-resolver attempt enforcement**. The exact server-side throttling code was not available.\n\nA robust implementation would generate high-entropy single-use reset tokens, expire and invalidate them correctly, rate-limit failed reset attempts **inside** the reset workflow (including aliases in the same GraphQL operation), and bound per-request execution complexity. Hiding schema introspection does not address any part of this chain."
